NNAAMMEE
     mmaannppaatthh — display search path for manual pages

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmaannppaatthh [--LLddqq]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmaannppaatthh utility determines the user's manual search path from the
     user's PATH, and local configuration files.  This result is echoed to the
     standard output.

     --LL  Output manual locales list instead of the manual path.

     --dd  Print extra debugging information.

     --qq  Suppresses warning messages.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     The mmaannppaatthh utility constructs the manual path from two sources:
     1.   From each component of the user's PATH for the first of:
          --   _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e_/_m_a_n
          --   _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e_/_M_A_N
          --   If pathname ends with /bin: _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e_/_._._/_s_h_a_r_e_/_m_a_n and
              _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e_/_._._/_m_a_n
     2.   The configuration files listed in the _F_I_L_E_S section for _M_A_N_P_A_T_H en‐
          tries.
     The information from these locations is then concatenated together.

     If the --LL flag is set, the mmaannppaatthh utility will search the configuration
     files listed in the _F_I_L_E_S section for _M_A_N_L_O_C_A_L_E entries.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     The following environment variables affect the execution of mmaannppaatthh:

     MANLOCALES  If set with the --LL flag, causes the utility to display a
                 warning and the value, overriding any other configuration
                 found on the system.

     MANPATH     If set, causes the utility to display a warning and the
                 value, overriding any other configuration found on the sys‐
                 tem.

     PATH        Influences the manual path as described in the _I_M_P_L_E_M_E_N_T_A_T_I_O_N
                 _N_O_T_E_S.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/man.conf                System configuration file.
     /usr/local/etc/man.d/*.conf  Local configuration files.

